        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Buraco Brazilian',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Buraco (also known as Canastra, Biriba, or Perida) is a four-player partnership card game from Brazil related to Canasta. Players meld sequences of three or more consecutive cards of a suit, aiming to create canastas (sequences of 7+ cards). The game uses two 52-card decks with four jokers (108 cards total).',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Buraco originated in the Rio de la Plata region of South America (Argentina and Uruguay) in the 1940s and remains widely played in Brazil. It is closely related to Canasta and similar to Samba in its melding mechanics. In Brazil the game is also known as Canastra, Biriba, or Perida in different regions.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 players in two fixed partnerships (partners sit opposite)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '108-card pack: two standard 52-card decks plus four wild jokers (curingÃ£o). Twos (curinga) can be used as wild cards or natural twos.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '108-card deck, score sheet, table space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: "Player to dealer's right cuts and forms two 11-card piles (mortos) set aside. Dealer deals 11 cards to each player from remaining pack. Remaining 42 cards form draw pile (monte). Discard pile (lixo/bagaÃ§o) starts empty.",
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
