    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Buraco originated in the Rio de la Plata region of South America (Argentina and Uruguay) in the 1940s and remains widely played in Brazil. It is closely related to Canasta and similar to Samba in its melding mechanics. In Brazil the game is also known as Canastra, Biriba, or Perida in different regions.',
      originCountries: [
        'Argentina',
        'Uruguay',
        'Brazil',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1940s: Game originated in South America',
        'Mid-late 20th century: Became popular throughout Brazil',
        '1990s: Italian variation Burraco became extremely popular in Italy',
        'Modern era: Digital versions and online play available',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game evolved from Canasta with unique features like the morto (extra hand) mechanic. A similar game Burako is played in Argentina using tiles instead of cards. Since the 1990s, the Italian variation Burraco has gained massive popularity.',
      cultural: 'Buraco is one of the most popular card games in Brazil, commonly played socially and in clubs. The game has regional variations including Buraco Aberto (open) and Buraco Fechado (closed).',
    },
